iscarding one would break a potential pung/pair, which is bad.  \n\nGiven tile counts from unshown: W1 (2 left), W3 (3 left), B4 (3 left) are relatively safe to keep for pung draws. W8 and B8 are less useful. Between W8 and B8, which is more dangerous to discard? Looking at opponents' melds:  \n- Next player: Chow B6-B7-B8 (so B8 is used), Chow T5-T6-T7.  \n- Across: Pung B5, Chow T7-T8-T9, Chow T6-T7-T8.  \n- Previous: No melds.  \n\nB8 is already in a Chow by next player, so discarding B8 might be less risky (since one B8 is already out). W8 has not appeared in melds. Also, from history: Previous player played W8 once, others not. W8 might be safer to hold? Actually, discarding B8 could be safer because B8 is already in a Chow, reducing its value for others.  \n\nBut for my hand, keeping B8 offers no strong benefit. W8 at least connects to W6/W7/W9, but I have none. Both are weak. I'll discard B8 to keep W8 for potential Chow if I draw W7/W9.  \n\n**Reason**: Discarding B8 preserves pairs of W1, W3, B4 for potential pungs, and keeps W8 which has slightly more Chow potential given W7 abundance (4 left). B8 is already exposed in a Chow, making it a safer discard.  \n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
